 Q1.
 At-Least-Once Delivery means Kafka guarantees that every message will be processed at least one time. No messages are lost, but sometimes the same message may be processed more than once.
it Works like
 Consumer reads a message
 Consumer processes the message
 Consumer commits the offset
If the consumer crashes after processing but before committing the offset, Kafka thinks the message was never completed.
When the consumer restarts, Kafka sends the same message again.
Q2.
Most companies use at-least-once delivery with idempotent processing because duplicate messages do not affect the final result. This provides the same business outcome as exactly-once delivery while being much simpler to implement.
Q3.
Payment processing requires exactly-once delivery because duplicate processing can charge customers multiple times. At-least-once delivery is not acceptable since replayed messages could create financial loss and compliance issues

